\ (y+1)^{2}-1-2[(x+2)^{2}-4] & =13 \\\\ (y+1)^{2}-1-2(x+2)^{2}+8 & =13 \\\\ (y+1)^{2}-2(x+2)^{2} & =13+1-8 \\\\ (y+1)^{2}-2(x+2)^{2} & =6 \\\\ \\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{6}-\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{3} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the center is $(-2,-1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366323 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186366324 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366323 #edb/ref 1069 "q-78862" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366323 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366323 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the center of the hyperbola $\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{3}-\\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{2}=1?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366323 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "801e2c36-b0ba-5a56-b56f-a1984ab99d04" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366323 #edb/ref 1074 "The equation of a horizontal hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that the given equation can be rewritten as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{3}-\\frac{(y-(-4))^{2}}{2}=1\n$$\n\nIn this case, $h=1$ and $k=-4.$\n\nTherefore, the center of the hyperbola is $(1,-4).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366324 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366325 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366324 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366326 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366324 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366327 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366324 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366328 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366324 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186366329 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366324 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366324 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366324 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186366327 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366324 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ba745298-dd2a-56a7-9fe5-4138626a285a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366325 #edb/ref 1080 "(3,2)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186366325 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] 